Here the neighboring comparisons are handled with `every`. The callback must be true for every index from `0` through `n - 2`. A value greater than its next value means the array is not sorted.

The last element needs no next-element check, which is why the loop condition stops at `n - 2`. Equal neighboring values are allowed.

For an input like `[5]`, there are no pairs to reject, and `every` correctly gives `true`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    arraySortedOrNot(arr, n) {
        return Array.from({ length: Math.max(0, n - 1) }, (_, index) => index)
            .every(index => arr[index] <= arr[index + 1]);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)` because the index list is created

The recursive function checks one neighboring pair at a time. If the current pair is in the wrong order, it returns `False`. Otherwise, it moves to the next index until the end is reached.

A short dry run for `[2, 2, 6]` is: compare `2` and `2`, then compare `2` and `6`. Both pass, so the final result is `True`.

This keeps the input unchanged. The extra space comes from the recursive calls, so this is mainly a simple alternative rather than the most space-saving version.

```python
class Solution:
    def arraySortedOrNot(self, arr, n):
        def check(index):
            if index >= n - 1:
                return True
            if arr[index] > arr[index + 1]:
                return False
            return check(index + 1)

        return check(0)
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack

Instead of checking only neighbors, this version checks every pair of positions. In a sorted array, an earlier value must never be greater than a later value. Finding one such pair is enough to return `false`.

A quick dry run on `[1, 3, 2]` finds `3 > 2`, so the result is `false`. For `[1, 2, 3]`, no invalid pair appears and the method returns `true`.

The code does not modify the array. Empty and one element inputs have no pair to break the rule, so they return `true`.

```csharp
class Solution
{
    public bool arraySortedOrNot(int[] arr, int n)
    {
        for (int left = 0; left < n; left++)
        {
            for (int right = left + 1; right < n; right++)
            {
                if (arr[left] > arr[right])
                {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

A sorted array only needs to keep each value no bigger than the value after it. The loop checks neighboring elements and stops as soon as it finds a decrease.

For example, with `[1, 2, 2, 5]`, every comparison is valid, so the method returns `true`. With `[1, 4, 3]`, the comparison `4 > 3` makes it return `false`.

The array is only read, so the input is not changed. An empty array and a one element array are also treated as sorted.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool arraySortedOrNot(int arr[], int n) {
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (arr[i - 1] > arr[i]) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

This version makes a sorted copy of the input and compares it with the original array. If both arrays match at every index, the original was already sorted.

The copy is sorted, not the given array, so the input stays unchanged. This is a little less efficient than checking neighbors directly, but it is easy to understand.

Edge cases such as `n = 0` or `n = 1` work because the comparison loop has no invalid access.

```java
class Solution {
    boolean arraySortedOrNot(int[] arr, int n) {
        int[] sorted = java.util.Arrays.copyOf(arr, n);
        java.util.Arrays.sort(sorted);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (arr[i] != sorted[i]) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)`

